problems.problem:
**The distant giant**

In an article entitled "Evidence for a distant giant planet in the Solar System", which appeared on 20 January 2016, two researchers hypothesise the existence of a giant planet at the edge of the Solar System. This object, according to their calculations, should travel on a rather eccentric orbit ($e = 0.6$) with semi-major axis $a = 700$ AU and have a mass of about 10 Earth masses. Assuming that the density of this object is equal to that of Neptune and that its surface reflects 80% of the visible light coming from the Sun, calculate:

1) the orbital period of the planet;
2) its distance from the Sun at perihelion and at aphelion;
3) the angular size of the planet at perihelion and at aphelion as seen from the Sun;
4) the visual magnitude observed from the Earth when the planet is at opposition and its distance from the Sun is 700 AU.

problems.problem_orig:
**Il gigante lontano**

In un articolo dal titolo "Evidence for a distant giant planet in the Solar System" apparso il 20 gennaio 2016, due ricercatori ipotizzano l'esistenza di un pianeta gigante ai confini del Sistema Solare. Tale oggetto, secondo i loro calcoli, dovrebbe percorrere un'orbita piuttosto eccentrica ($e = 0.6$) con semiasse maggiore $a = 700$ UA e possedere una massa pari a circa 10 masse terrestri. Supponendo che la densità di tale oggetto sia pari a quella di Nettuno e che la sua superficie rifletta l'80% della luce visibile proveniente dal Sole, calcolare:

1) il periodo orbitale del pianeta;
2) la sua distanza dal Sole al perielio e all'afelio;
3) la dimensione angolare del pianeta al perielio e all'afelio visto dal Sole;
4) la magnitudine visuale osservata dalla Terra quando il pianeta è in opposizione e la sua distanza dal Sole è di 700 UA.

problems.problem:
**The Hipparcos II satellite**

In the 1990s the Hipparcos satellite, in orbit around the Earth, measured with the parallax method the distance of about 126000 stars up to a limiting distance of 200 pc. Among them we recall $\alpha$ Centauri, whose parallax is $\pi_{Cen} = 0''.75$, and Barnard's Star, whose parallax is $\pi_{Barnard} = 0''.55$. A new satellite, Hipparcos II, with the same technical characteristics, is now being planned, to be sent however into orbit around the planet Saturn.

Calculate, in the case of Hipparcos II:

a) the parallaxes that will be measured for $\alpha$ Centauri and for Barnard's Star;
b) the new limiting distance in pc for which the same accuracy of measurements as those made by the Hipparcos satellite will be obtained;
c) the number of measurable stars, assuming a homogeneous stellar density;
d) after how long we will have the first measurements with the maximum attainable precision;
e) how much a Saturnian parsec is worth compared with the terrestrial one, in AU.

Neglect the satellite-Saturn distance and consider circular orbits.

problems.problem_orig:
**Il satellite Hipparcos II**

Negli anni '90 il satellite Hipparcos, in orbita intorno alla Terra, ha misurato con il metodo delle parallassi la distanza di circa 126000 stelle fino a una distanza limite di 200 pc. Tra queste ricordiamo $\alpha$ Centauri, la cui parallasse è $\pi_{Cen} = 0''.75$, e la Stella di Barnard, la cui parallasse è $\pi_{Barnard} = 0''.55$. Ora si sta progettando il nuovo satellite Hipparcos II, con le stesse caratteristiche tecniche, da mandare però in orbita intorno al pianeta Saturno.

Calcola nel caso di Hipparcos II:

a) le parallassi che verranno misurate per $\alpha$ Centauri e per la Stella di Barnard;
b) la nuova distanza limite in pc per cui si avrà la stessa accuratezza delle misure effettuate dal satellite Hipparcos;
c) il numero di stelle misurabili, considerando una densità stellare omogenea;
d) dopo quanto tempo avremo le prime misure con la massima precisione ottenibile;
e) quanto vale un pc saturniano rispetto a quello terrestre in UA.

Trascura la distanza satellite-Saturno e considera orbite circolari.

problems.problem:
**Mission to Mars**

As we saw in one of the problems of the Interregional Competition, Pippo is leaving for the planet Mars. However the TASA (TopoliniA Space Agency) does not have much money available and must use a launch vehicle as cheap as possible. One of the most "economical" ways to send a spacecraft from the Earth to Mars is to use a "Hohmann transfer orbit" (named after the German engineer Wolfgang Hohmann who first proposed this trajectory in 1925). The spacecraft is placed on an elliptical orbit (dashed curve in the figure) which has its perihelion on the Earth's orbit and its aphelion on the orbit of Mars. If the spacecraft is launched when the Earth is at point "T", it will reach Mars when the latter is at point "M". Once the correct initial velocity has been imparted to it, the spacecraft will continue without further propulsion, exploiting only the gravity of the Sun.

Calculate:

1) the time taken by the spacecraft to reach Mars starting from "T";
2) the eccentricity of the orbit followed by the spacecraft;
3) the velocity, in addition to the orbital one at the Earth, that must be imparted to the spacecraft to insert it into the transfer orbit;
4) the velocity that the spacecraft will have when it meets Mars at point "M";
5) the change of velocity that Pippo will have to impart to the spacecraft to insert it into the orbit of Mars: will he have to brake or accelerate, and by how much?
6) calling "$M_1$" the position of Mars at the moment of launch, the value of the angle $M\hat{S}M_1$ formed by the lines joining the Sun with the two positions of Mars; identify with a drawing the point "$M_1$" on the orbit;
7) calling "$T_1$" the position of the Earth at the moment of arrival of the spacecraft at Mars, the value of the angle $M\hat{S}T_1$ formed by the lines joining the Sun with the positions of Mars and of the Earth; identify with a drawing the point "$T_1$" on the orbit.

For the solution consider for the Earth and Mars circular orbits lying in the same plane and neglect the gravitational effects of the Earth and of Mars, considering therefore only the gravity of the Sun.

