problems.problem:
**Observing the Sun**

Two observers in the northern hemisphere, both at sea level, are at the same latitude and at longitudes $\lambda_1 = 15^\circ 16' 30''$ and $\lambda_2 = 195^\circ 16' 30''$. For both of them the upper culmination of the Sun occurs south of the zenith, the horizon is unobstructed and the sky is perfectly clear. At a certain instant the mean solar time of the first observer is $T_{sm1} = 12^\mathrm{h}\,00^\mathrm{m}$. Neglecting the value of the equation of time:

1. calculate the mean solar time at the same instant for the second observer;

2. describe at which latitudes and on which days of the year the two observers should be so that both can see the Sun when $T_{sm1} = 12^\mathrm{h}\,00^\mathrm{m}$;

3. describe at which latitudes and on which days of the year the two observers should be so that both cannot see the Sun when $T_{sm1} = 12^\mathrm{h}\,00^\mathrm{m}$.

